\begin{abstract}
We report a diagnostic phenomenon in which a coarse-grained entity observable shows no non-monotonic extremum, while a fine-grained relational observable exhibits a sharp non-monotonic extremum. In the Sierpi\'{n}ski fractal graph ($N=15$), high-resolution scanning (201 points) reveals a W-shaped double-valley structure in the standard deviation of edge correlation fluctuations: a primary valley at $s=0.275$ with depth $0.1111$ ($30.3\%$ relative to the left shoulder) and a secondary valley at $s\approx0.550$ with depth $0.136$, separated by a central peak at $s=0.400$. The default diagnosis (mean magnetization) shows a smooth U-shaped background with no corresponding extrema. We designate this structure the \textbf{Li-Draho\v{s}-Sultanov (LDS) W-structure}. In the PXP model ($L=18$), the standard deviation of the half-chain entanglement spectrum jumps by $+7\%$ at chemical potential $\mu\approx1.5$, while the mean absolute spin correlation decreases monotonically; a half-chain entanglement entropy calculation excludes quantum many-body scar-state crossing as the origin. Rastislav Draho\v{s} independently replicated the valley and confirmed it as a localized defect mode. Marat Sultanov's TAT-7 blind test confirmed the structure as a smooth modulation, not a sharp transition. Strong evidence is currently confined to the fractal graph; other topologies produce weaker signals to be analyzed separately. All data, codes, and protocols are publicly available.
\end{abstract}

