\subsection{Excluding known interpretations}

\textbf{Not a quantum phase transition.} The ground-state energy gap remains non-zero throughout the scan interval.

\textbf{Not an entanglement entropy singularity.} The enhanced diagnosis uses the standard deviation of the entanglement spectrum, which is sensitive to the spectral tail, not the Shannon entropy, which is dominated by the bulk.

\textbf{Not a scar-state crossing.} The chemical potential defect modulates the entire relational field, not just the scar subspace.

\textbf{Finite-size trend.} At $L=14$, no signal; at $L=16$, weak; at $L=18$, clear. The signal strengthens with system size, indicating emergent behavior.

\subsection{Tail-slope verification}

The tail slope of the entanglement spectrum changed by $0.0313$ at $\mu\approx1.5$, exceeding the maximum background change of $0.0290$. This confirms a global reorganization through the entire spectrum.

\subsection{Excluding quantum many-body scar-state crossing}
\label{sec:qmbs}

To exclude the possibility that the jump in the enhanced diagnosis originates from quantum many-body scar (QMBS) state crossing, we computed the half-chain entanglement entropy $S_{\text{ent}}$ over the same $\mu$ interval. The full data are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:qmbs} and Table~\ref{tab:qmbs}. $S_{\text{ent}}$ rises smoothly from $0.1742$ at $\mu=0.00$ to a peak of $0.2004$ at $\mu=0.57$, then decreases monotonically to $0.0860$ at $\mu=2.00$. At $\mu=1.57$---where the enhanced diagnosis jumps by $+7.0\%$---$S_{\text{ent}}$ decreases smoothly from $0.1441$ to $0.1283$, showing no anomaly whatsoever. This definitively excludes QMBS scar-state crossing as the origin of the Silent Discordance signal in the PXP model.

