\section{Independent replication in a self-built fractal system}

\subsection{Setup}

We generate a system from a Sierpi\'{n}ski fractal graph (level 2): $N=15$ nodes, $27$ edges, $32768$-dimensional Hilbert space. A contradiction edge $(0,2)$ is implanted, connecting vertices of two different sub-triangles. The strength $s$ is scanned from $0$ to $3.0$.

\subsection{L1 Sierpi\'{n}ski gasket results}
\label{sec:l1}

We first examined the smallest non-trivial Sierpi\'{n}ski gasket ($L=1$, $N=6$, $6$ edges) using exact diagonalization. The contradiction edge is placed at $(0,2)$, connecting the two vertices of adjacent sub-triangles. The enhanced diagnosis (edge correlation standard deviation) shows a shallow valley at $s=0.50$, dropping from $0.4596$ to $0.4459$ (a relative decrease of $3.0\%$), while the default diagnosis (mean absolute magnetization) decreases from $0.0123$ to $0.0009$ at $s=0.75$ and becomes exactly zero for $s\geq1.00$. Silent Discordance already exists at the minimal fractal scale, although the signal is weak. This establishes a scaling trend: the signal strengthens from L1 (shallow valley) to L2 (deep W-structure).

